                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose & prioritise stakeholders`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The NGO names three things: <strong>water and effluent</strong> at two drought-region plants, <strong>precarious rider pay</strong>, and <strong>deforestation-linked sourcing</strong>. Using a materiality lens, decide which stakeholder priority leads. The trap is that the loudest pressure — retail customers and the trending hashtag — is <em>not</em> the most material issue. Conscious-capitalism strategy creates value by <strong>aligning</strong> stakeholders, not ranking them by who can hurt revenue fastest. Find the priority that serves several stakeholders at once: water and credible sourcing protect farmers, communities, retailers and investors together.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the CSR initiatives to fund`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Allocate the <strong>R$28M</strong> across the candidates. Funding everything credibly would cost <strong>~R$52M</strong>, so you must prioritise. The water-treatment retrofit and smallholder traceability are <strong>high-materiality, value-creating</strong> spend that answers the named risks; the rider scheme is visible and moderate; the community programme is cheap, popular and photogenic but <em>immaterial</em>. The classic error is spreading the budget thinly across feel-good projects that photograph well and leave the effluent and sourcing risks untouched. Distinguish substantive CSR-ROI from spend that just looks good.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Set the reporting & disclosure scope`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Decide <strong>what and how</strong> to report. A recognised framework (GRI) plus third-party <strong>assurance</strong> converts claims into credible disclosure; the same glossy unassured brochure is what got you accused of greenwashing. Choose the <strong>boundary</strong>: own-operations-only is comfortable but hides the supply chain where the deforestation and farmer issues live — and the NGO already has that data. Broad, verified disclosure builds credibility but exposes weaknesses; narrow, flattering disclosure is cheaper but reads as spin. One plant's effluent numbers can't be verified in time — how candid will you be?`,
