                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_overbudget:       `<strong>You designed a plan you cannot afford.</strong> You committed more than R$28M, so the CFO cuts it arbitrarily and you lose control of which priorities survive. Prioritisation is the strategy here — decide what you consciously choose to defer, and defend that to the farmers you didn't fund.`,
                    coach_r1_material:      `<strong>That is the most material priority.</strong> Water/effluent and deforestation-linked sourcing are where your real risk and real impact live — and fixing them serves farmers, plant communities, retailers and investors at once. You led with substance, not the hashtag.`,
                    coach_r1_farmers:       `<strong>Farmers and traceability matter</strong> — and this is close to the material core. But the named effluent risk at the two drought-region plants is still the sharpest exposure. Make sure water sits alongside sourcing at the top of your materiality matrix.`,
                    coach_r1_riders:       `<strong>Rider welfare is real and visible</strong> — but it is not the most material issue the NGO named. Lead with it and the water and sourcing time bombs stay unfunded for the day-60 follow-up. Visibility is not the same as materiality.`,
                    coach_r1_retail:        `<strong>You put the loudest pressure first.</strong> Retail and the trending hashtag are urgent, but they are salience, not materiality. The NGO's follow-up in 60 days will be about the water and sourcing you left under-funded. What does your materiality matrix actually say is most material, and why did the hashtag win?`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp, disciplined diagnosis.</strong> You used double materiality to lead with water and sourcing — the issues that score highest on impact to the business AND impact on people and planet. That is conscious capitalism as strategy, not philanthropy.`,
                    coach_r1_salience:      `<strong>Salience is not materiality.</strong> Ranking by who is shouting loudest hands your strategy to the news cycle. The discipline is to rank by impact — on the business and on people and planet — not by volume.`,
                    coach_r1_power:         `<strong>Ranking by stakeholder power is the old playbook.</strong> Conscious capitalism creates value by aligning an integrated set of stakeholders, not by serving whoever can hurt revenue fastest. The most powerful voice is not the most material issue.`,
                    coach_r1_nolens:        `<strong>With no lens you will chase every pressure.</strong> That is exactly the “broad, shallow” pattern that created the credibility gap. A materiality lens is what separates the substantive priority from the noise.`,
                    coach_r1_lowhonesty:    `<strong>Decision logged.</strong>`,
                    coach_r2_materialroi:   `<strong>Concentrated on the material work — that is CSR-ROI.</strong> Funding the water retrofit and traceability properly creates real, verifiable stakeholder value and answers exactly the risks the NGO named, rather than spreading the budget for the photo.`,
                    coach_r2_thin:          `<strong>You funded eight feel-good things and fixed none of them.</strong> The photogenic community spend outweighs the material work, so the effluent at the two named plants is untouched. A wide, shallow portfolio is not strategy — it is spreading PR evenly. Concentrate on the material, value-creating initiatives.`,
                    coach_r2_spread:        `<strong>Spreading it evenly leaves the material risk under-funded.</strong> Every group gets something visible, but the water and sourcing issues — the ones that actually create or destroy value — never get the concentration they need. Prioritise, and defend what you chose not to fund.`,
                    coach_r2_cheapest:      `<strong>Cheapest-and-most-photogenic is the greenwashing portfolio.</strong> You optimised for coverage, not for stakeholder value. The school and the tree-planting photograph beautifully and fix nothing the NGO named. That is precisely the spend that created the credibility gap.`,
                    coach_r2_underspend:    `<strong>You barely touched the R$28M.</strong> Under-spending in a credibility crisis signals you do not take the material risk seriously — and leaves obvious value on the table. The budget is a constraint to prioritise within, not to hoard.`,
                    coach_r3_credible:      `<strong>This is credible disclosure.</strong> GRI plus assurance plus a full supply-chain boundary converts your real action into verifiable claims — and it covers exactly where the deforestation and farmer issues live. That is what closes the credibility gap.`,
                    coach_r3_brochure:      `<strong>The same glossy brochure got you here.</strong> Real action reported with the unassured comms that started the greenwashing accusation still reads as spin. The NGO and investors both said the credibility gap is the problem. What does a recognised framework and assurance buy you that a brochure can't?`,
                    coach_r3_noassurance:   `<strong>Without assurance your numbers are just claims.</strong> Third-party assurance is the antidote to the credibility gap — self-declared metrics, however true, are exactly what the NGO is challenging. Substance plus unverifiable reporting still reads as spin.`,
                    coach_r3_boundary:      `<strong>Own-operations-only is the comfortable, useless boundary.</strong> You scoped out the supply chain — where the deforestation and farmer issues live and where the NGO already has data. When your report omits it, the omission becomes the next headline. Selective disclosure is itself a greenwashing risk.`,
                    coach_r3_selective:     `<strong>Hiding the unflattering numbers is the trap.</strong> Disclosing the effluent data you can't fully verify, with a note on what you are doing about it, is far cheaper than being caught omitting it. The NGO already has the number.`,
                    coach_r4_calibrated:    `<strong>Calibrated and credible.</strong> A funded, verified, under-promised plan with evidence is what rebuilds trust durably — and it gives the journalist nothing to fact-check against you in 60 days. This is recovery without re-triggering the backlash.`,
                    coach_r4_confident:     `<strong>“A clear leader” is already stretching it.</strong> A lightly-evidenced confident claim drifts back toward the overclaim that started this. Tie every claim to a verifiable metric, or the journalist on the panel writes it down to fact-check.`,
                    coach_r4_overclaim:     `<strong>That is the overclaim that started the crisis.</strong> Calling Verano “Brazil's most sustainable food company” off a 45-day plan is the bigger adjective, not the credible delivery. The journalist just wrote it down to fact-check at day 60. Trust recovers from under-promising, not from a louder claim.`,
                    coach_r4_pr:            `<strong>A PR campaign on top of the plan is the re-greenwashing loop.</strong> Polishing the message instead of integrating the substance is exactly what the NGO is watching for. Let the verified plan speak; do not wrap it in spin.`,
                    coach_r4_engage:        `<strong>Pre-empting the NGO is the conscious move.</strong> Sharing the verified plan and the unflattering data before the day-60 report turns the watchdog from an attacker into a witness to your credibility. That is how you defuse the follow-up.`,
                    coach_r4_defend:        `<strong>A defensive rebuttal deepens the trap.</strong> Pushing back hard with PR is the classic greenwashing escalation — the NGO has the data and a follow-up report ready. Engage with the evidence, do not fight the messenger.`,

